      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "The authors specifically explored the association of obesity (based on body mass index [BMI]) and the risk of developing acute kidney injury after cardiopulmonary bypass (AKICPB)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Single-center retrospective study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Academic medical center."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS AND INTERVENTIONS",
            "content": "After IRB approval, 376 eligible adults who underwent cardiac surgery with cardiopulmonary bypass between 2006-2010 were included in the final retrospective analysis. Patients undergoing \"off-pump\" procedures, cardiac transplants, repair of congenital heart disease, and patients with preoperative circulatory assist devices were excluded."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The overall incidence of developing AKICPB in this population based on the Acute Kidney Injury Network serum creatinine criteria was 39% (147 of 376). Among the BMI classes, the morbidly obese cohort (ie, BMI>40 kg/m(2)) had a significantly greater risk of developing AKICPB than those in lower BMI classes. BMI>40 kg/m(2) was significantly associated with development of AKICPB even after accounting for covariates (ie, diabetes mellitus, hypertension, age, severity of illness, and CPB time) (overall p = 0.018). The odds ratio of AKICPB in the BMI>40 kg/m(2) cohort relative to BMI<25 kg/m(2) was 2.39 (95% CI: 0.98, 5.82; p = 0.055), with no significant difference in risk of developing AKICPB among the 4 lower BMI classes."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "BMI>40 kg/m(2) is associated with a significantly higher risk of developing of AKICPB."
          }
        ]
      },
